    "math_academy_id": "q-12921",
    "knowledge_point_id": "b428331b-4916-5b2c-8aa6-89a2358cdef0",
    "knowledge_point": "Finding the Indefinite Integral of a Exponential-Trigonometric Expression Using Integration by Parts",
    "problem": "Calculate $\\int {e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x.$",
    "worked_solution": "To use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{3x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3{e}^{3x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int {e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)}{5})-\\int (-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)}{5})(3{e}^{3x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)}{5}+\\frac{3}{5}\\int {e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)}{5}+\\frac{3}{5}J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we can use integration by parts to evaluate\n\n$$\nJ=\\int {e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{3x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3{e}^{3x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J & =\\int {e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)}{5})-\\int (\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)}{5})(3{e}^{3x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)}{5}-\\frac{3}{5}\\int {e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)}{5}-\\frac{3}{5}I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this back into our equation for $I,$ we can subsequently solve for $I.$ We remember to add a constant of integration $C$ at the end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =-\\frac{{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)}{5}+\\frac{3}{5}J \\\\ I & =-\\frac{{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)}{5}+\\frac{3}{5}(\\frac{{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)}{5}-\\frac{3}{5}I) \\\\ I & =-\\frac{{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)}{5}+\\frac{3{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)}{25}-\\frac{9}{25}I \\\\ \\frac{34}{25}I & =\\frac{3{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)-5{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)}{25} \\\\ I & =\\frac{{e}^{3x}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x))}{34}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$",
